  "rsrpHint": "RSRP — Reference Signal Received Power.\n\nThe average power of the cell's reference signals, measured in dBm. It reflects raw signal strength.\n\nTypical range: about −80 dBm (excellent) down to −120 dBm (very weak). Higher (closer to zero) is better.",
  "rsrqHint": "RSRQ — Reference Signal Received Quality.\n\nSignal quality in dB, factoring in interference and network load alongside strength.\n\nTypical range: about −3 dB (excellent) down to −20 dB (poor). Higher is better.",
  "sinrHint": "SINR — Signal to Interference-plus-Noise Ratio.\n\nHow much the wanted signal exceeds interference plus background noise, in dB.\n\nHigher is better: above ~20 dB is excellent, around 0 dB or below is poor.",
  "pciHint": "PCI — Physical Cell ID.\n\nA number (0–503 on LTE) that identifies the serving cell on the radio interface. Neighbouring cells use different PCIs so the phone can tell them apart.",
  "earfcnHint": "EARFCN — E-UTRA Absolute Radio Frequency Channel Number.\n\nIdentifies the exact carrier frequency the device is using; it maps to a specific LTE band and channel.",
  "estDistHint": "Estimated distance to the cell tower.\n\nDerived from signal strength (RSRP) using a radio propagation model. It is a very rough, order-of-magnitude indication only — not a precise measurement."
